n1994:   so the kernel restores it as the call's response reader, exactly as\n2091:   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n2098:   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n2099:   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\n2151:   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n2171:   restore function as a value.\"\n2186:   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n2226:   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n2230:   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n2234:   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n2236:   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n2238:  [state restore-fn]\n2256:          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n2261:   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n2265:   This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` with its restore made a\n2268:  [state restore-fn]\n2270:    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n2302:  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n2306:      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n2565:(defn handle-effect\n2575:   stream handle nor a restore closure is attached — `check-wait-set`\n    75\t- **K**: a vector of frames, innermost last. One frame kind for calls,\n    86\tin program order except that the two arms of a conditional write the\n   124\t`:regs` (non-negative integers, vectors of them), as the register\n   153\t\\to \\langle seg',\\,b,\\,\\{\\},\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,\\,W\\!\\restriction\\!L(pc{+}1),\\,rd)]\\rangle\\\\\n   159\t\\to \\mathrm{effect}(\\epsilon,\\ \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W\\!\\restriction\\!L(pc{+}1),\\,E,S,K\\rangle,\\ \\mathrm{deliver}(rd, tail))\n   164\tregisters live at the successor, `W↾L(pc+1)` (§8.1), together with the\n   191\t&blocked:&& \\to \\text{parked}\\ \\{seg,\\ pc{+}1,\\ E,\\ W\\!\\restriction\\!L(pc{+}1),\\ K,\\ \\mathrm{deliver}(rd,\\mathrm{false}),\\ \\text{cell}\\ c\\}\\\\\n   202\t`{eval-call, seg, pc+1, E, W↾L(pc+1), K, deliver(rd, false)}` under a\n   206\t**park**: the record `{seg, pc+1, E, W↾L(pc+1), K, deliver(rd, false)}`\n   215\t`κ = {seg, pc+1, E, W↾L(pc+1), K, deliver(rd, false)}` reified as data.\n   218\town destination, because `rd` is excluded from `W↾L(pc+1)`.\n   254\t              | effect(rd)\n   258\t                expr-into(rd) [:jump Lend]\n   259\t                Lalt: expr-into(rd)\n   262\teffect(rd)  ::= expr(args…) [<stream-or-ffi-op> rd …]\n   265\texpr-into(rd) ::= an expr whose last instruction writes rd\n   268\t`expr-into(rd)` is how both arms of a conditional write the same\n   275\tA register has at most one definition on any path. The two arms of a\n   301\t   on entry, before its test, and both arms are lowered `expr-into(rd)`.\n   307\t3. **Every expression receives a destination**, used or not. There are\n   311\t4. **Tail flag**: copied from `:yin/tail?` as the front end marks it,\n   368\t| explicit park | `:park rd` | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)` | `rd` ← resume value |\n   369\t| blocked read | `:stream-next rd c` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)` | `rd` ← read value; nil on `end`; `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n   370\t| blocked write | `:stream-put rd s v` → `full` | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)`; retained value `W[v]` in pending | `rd` ← written value on retry `ok` |\n   371\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call rd op args` → `ok` | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)` | `rd` ← correlated `ok` value; `error` raises |\n   373\t| effectful call, non-tail | `:call rd f args false` whose operator yields a blocking effect | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)` | `rd` ← the effect's resume value, per its kind |\n   375\t| link request / response / install | the `:module/require` effect's three wait states | `pc+1` | `W↾L(pc+1)` | `rd` ← the module symbol on `linked`; refusal raises |\n   395\t :yin.k/window   {r (encoded) …}         ; W↾L(pc), sorted by r\n   406\trecomputes `L(pc)` from A (§8.1) and refuses a window with a register\n   412\taccordingly: `:regs` becomes `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\n   422\t`:stack-effect` with the per-pc **def and use sets** and `L(pc)` derived\n   425\tsafepoint, produce every register in `L(pc)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's\n   486\t- `L(pc)`, the **live-in set** at `pc`: registers read at or after `pc`\n   504\t   `:closure rd params body-pc` is the unique site owning body `body-pc`\n   512\t2. **Allocate per body.** Virtual registers are definition-ordered by\n   521\t3. **Fill `live`.** For every boundary instruction (`:call`, stream\n   523\t   operand is `L(pc+1)` mapped to physical registers, which is what the\n   603\tuntil phase 8. (Phase 1 is in flight as of this writing.)\n   648\t3. **Whether `L(pc)` should be carried in UCF frames as a checkable\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1791510000000-architect-semantic-register-vm.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; sed -n '490,595p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '640,670p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '298,319p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '510,590p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1791510000000-architect-semantic-register-vm.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; sed -n '490,595p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '640,670p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '298,319p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '510,590p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\"","aggregated_output":"I’ll read the architecture rules, VM contracts, and landed tests, then assess the proposal’s defects, specification gaps, and migration cost. I’ll keep the review read-only.\nThe proposal can preserve CESK and the six invariants, but two claims need correction: register-to-stack lowering is not generally a local expansion, and R1’s allocator is embedded in its expression walk. The register-window format also needs a precise liveness and resume-destination contract.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-09 18:06:09 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 01:06:09 Asia/Bangkok\n\nThe register-shaped canonical VM is architecturally viable. The proposal understates the lowering and continuation work, however, and needs tighter canonical-form rules before implementation.\n\nRead-only review; no files edited or tests run. File references below identify the current contracts affected by the proposal.\n\n| Severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High — architectural defect in claimed derivation | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc:33` | The existing inverse uses a recoverable expression grammar. A general register vector cannot be translated to today’s operand stack one instruction at a time: previously computed operands may be buried, reused, or needed across branches. | Restrict canonical vectors to a validated structured grammar and specify a whole-body stack lowering, or introduce explicit stack storage/access instructions and version that format. |\n| High — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:490` | Fresh destinations and shared arm destinations do not uniquely determine numbering, body scope, or permitted joins. | Define minting order, body/register scope, conditional destination reservation, body discovery order, and definite-assignment validation. |\n| High — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md:312` | Existing semantic return frames rely on stack bases; register return requires a destination and saved caller window. | Specify active, parked, reified, return, and tail-effect states separately, including exactly-once result delivery. |\n| High — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:672` | A register window needs a canonical membership rule. “No live sets” in code does not eliminate liveness from continuation semantics. | Derive and validate window membership from A; keep the derived live set outside A’s persisted instruction operands. |\n| High — identity correction | `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:908`; `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:95`; `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md:239` | H/R fetching, sharing, and emitter keys contradict owner ruling 1 independently of register shape. | Amend identity contracts immediately: requests, publication, pins, dependencies, and executable admission name A; H/R are derived checksums. |\n| Medium — implementation cost | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc:152` | R1 allocation is integrated with target-register-passing recursion and explicit expression-consumption events. | Replace its input walk; do not describe this as plugging a vector into an existing standalone allocator. |\n| Medium — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:438` | The semantic stamp covers grammar, transitions, restoration, and scheduling. | Publish a new semantic contract and changed UCF grammar/version. Decide b2/r2 retention separately using exact-byte evidence. |\n| Medium — implementation cost | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:866` | Landed transport validates exact semantic frame keys and reconstructs stack-based state. | Budget a transport/validator rewrite and full Stage D/E regression, beyond fixture re-encoding. |\n\n1. **Invariants**\n\n   All six can survive:\n\n   - **No hidden global state:** virtual-register counters must be scoped lowering data. Runtime windows, result destinations, and saved caller state must be explicit.\n   - **No implicit control flow:** branches, calls, returns, park/resume, and tail-result delivery remain explicit transitions.\n   - **No callbacks:** effects continue through the existing stream protocol.\n   - **No shared mutable state:** register windows are immutable machine-state values; captures must preserve existing isolation and cell semantics.\n   - **Interpretation separate from execution:** vectors remain passive data interpreted by loaders and evaluators.\n   - **No assumed graphs:** def/use edges, body ownership, lexical chains, and control-flow successors may be constructed from validated tuples. They must not depend on an unavailable AST or provenance table.\n\n   Virtual registers do introduce a derived def/use graph, but that is permissible when constructed explicitly. They introduce no necessary global state.\n\n   “Derive, don’t persist” supports keeping allocation, liveness tables, ownership indexes, and physical layouts out of A. Explicit register operands are executable syntax; they are not merely duplicated analysis metadata.\n\n2. **Identity**\n\n   **Yes, with a pinned lowering algorithm.** A can remain deterministic without depending on physical allocation or target scheduling. “AST and evaluation order alone” is too broad: opcode selection, defaults, tail flags, body placement, and register-minting conventions also determine the vector, just as instruction-layout conventions determine today’s vector.\n\n   The current rule is insufficient. Decide:\n\n   - Whether register IDs are segment-wide or body-local.\n   - Whether an expression’s destination is reserved before lowering its children or minted after them.\n   - How a conditional reserves its shared destination.\n   - Operator/operand order, consequent/alternate emission order, and queued lambda-body discovery order.\n   - Whether discarded expressions still receive destinations.\n   - Which moves, folds, or rewrites are permitted.\n\n   For example, reserving a call’s result before its operator versus afterward produces different numbering without changing evaluation order.\n\n   Call this **structured single-assignment syntax with exclusive arm definitions**, rather than ordinary SSA. An arm-shared destination has two static definitions. Validation must ensure that every reachable read has an assigned value and that branch definitions obey the structured join rule.\n\n   A remains an exact-vector identity, not an alpha-equivalence identity: named binders in A can change A while resolved H/R remain equal. Preserve that distinction.\n\n3. **Semantics**\n\n   This remains CESK. C may contain `⟨seg, pc, regs⟩`; CESK does not require an accumulator or operand stack.\n\n   Define a fresh window on closure entry. A non-tail call saves the caller’s window, environment, successor PC, and result destination in K. Return restores that state and writes the result once. A tail call discards the current activation and preserves the caller continuation.\n\n   Effects become easier to describe because operands and result destinations are explicit. Their stream outcomes, pending data, retries, correlation, errors, and scheduling remain unchanged in meaning.\n\n   Important decisions:\n\n   - **Park:** record the suspended operation’s destination, excluding its not-yet-produced value from the saved window.\n   - **Resume:** restore the captured window and deliver the supplied value to that destination.\n   - **Current-continuation:** capture the post-instruction continuation with an explicit delivery destination; avoid accidentally capturing the continuation value recursively in its own destination.\n   - **Effectful tail calls:** specify whether completion returns through K directly. Do not assume every suspended call resumes by writing a current-body register.\n   - **Gensym:** register minting is compile-time numbering; runtime gensym still advances the task’s explicit counter.\n   - **FFI:** retained requests and responses remain pending data, not register-map substitutes.\n   - **Define/Rule R:** write the source-register value through `engine/store-put`, then produce that value in the destination. Reserved-name refusal remains unchanged.\n   - **Module store-of:** preserve captured module-store context across calls, returns, captures, and migration. Changing C must not lose that context.\n\n4. **Safepoints**\n\n   The **semantic kinds survive**: explicit park, blocked read/write, sent/retained FFI, effectful calls, and linker request/response/install waits. Current-continuation remains a reified value operation, not a parking safepoint. Halt remains a result.\n\n   This is more than `St → window` notation. Rewrite the table’s state columns and distinguish write-result from tail-return delivery. No new external effect kind is inherently required, but delivery cases need explicit coverage.\n\n   A resume value means the operation’s completed result, delivered either:\n\n   - to the instruction’s designated destination; or\n   - through the preserved return continuation for a tail effect.\n\n   Define a window as a sorted sparse mapping of register IDs to values with an exact, code-derived membership rule. Liveness must account for branches, return, and capture. Reject missing, duplicate, out-of-scope, or inappropriate entries. Dead-value retention must not make otherwise equivalent captures differ or introduce avoidable non-portability.\n\n   Foreign-engine reconstruction must cover canonical PC, every required virtual value, E, K, destinations, pending state, and captured store context. A physical-slot map alone is insufficient when values are spilled, rematerialized, shared between locations, or represented by lexical frames.\n\n   Preserve `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` for admitted canonical frames. Replace stack-effect metadata with appropriate derived register use/def and delivery facts.\n\n5. **The two derivations**\n\n   **A → R:** resolution plus deterministic allocation is the right decomposition, but R1 is not presently a reusable vector allocator.\n\n   Changes to §4.1–4.6 include input validation, explicit body/owner reconstruction, removal of expression traversal, virtual-to-physical mapping, join handling, register-bank counts, lifetime/tie-break rules, PC relocation, and live-set filling. `body-liveness` is reusable in principle. The existing register-image lift must also change its output contract.\n\n   **A → H:** contest “local expansion.” For `(f (g x) y)`, the stack lowering must preserve `f` while evaluating the nested call, then preserve its result while evaluating `y`. Register instructions alone require a stack-placement strategy or recoverable expression structure to produce the existing stack operations.\n\n   Two viable choices:\n\n   - Validate an expression-structured canonical register grammar and lower whole bodies using that grammar.\n   - Accept more general register programs and define storage/access operations or another stackification strategy.\n\n   The first fits the current project more closely and offers the best chance of preserving H.\n\n   **Byte identity is achievable in principle, not guaranteed.** H can remain identical if lowering reconstructs today’s exact emission order, pushes, branches, bodies, and defaults. R can remain identical if allocation reproduces target-register reservation and release timing, including branch handling—not merely generic last-use linear scan.\n\n   **Do not automatically re-version b2/r2 because their input changes.** Keep them only if their descriptors and execution contracts remain unchanged. If opcode shapes, allocation contract, live operands, or control rules change, **re-version them plainly**. The semantic contract must change regardless.\n\n6. **Blast radius**\n\n   **Rewrite:**\n\n   - `yin.vm.semantic.md`: opcode/schema tables, validation, configuration, transitions, wait/return shapes, linearizer contract.\n   - UCF §7.3.2, §7.4, relevant value/census/restoration rules and acceptance matrix.\n   - V2 amendment semantic registers/frames; identity/layout sections across profiles.\n   - Row carrier §5.2, validation, census traversal and examples.\n   - Register design topology, input/lowering/lift contracts; targets identity and fetched-code path.\n   - `yin.vm`, `code`, `linearize`, `semantic`, `ucf`, `ucf.handoff`, `completion`, and the de Bruijn resolver/lowerer interfaces.\n   - Pipeline and linker publication/fetch/admission paths currently organized around H/R.\n   - Semantic, code, linearizer, continuation-invoke, safepoint, lift, handoff layout/validity/census, and cross-engine tests.\n\n   **Notational or focused edits, conditional on preserved native contracts:**\n\n   - Code-as-tuples segment instruction contract and two-loader validator interface; its AST row grammar can remain.\n   - UCF revision history and implementation plan.\n   - Stack/register native VM documentation and restore adapters.\n   - Blog Part Three: replace both the St explanation and any affected identity/topology claims.\n   - REPL/session composition, encoder loaders, telemetry and benchmark fixtures referencing the old shape.\n\n   **Corpora:** preserve source programs and expected results/errors/effect traces. Regenerate semantic vectors, A values, PCs, saved frames and transported bodies. Re-encode Stage D/E scenarios and affected handoff/checkpoint fixtures. Preserve H/R goldens only if byte identity is achieved.\n\n   **Untouched in semantic substance:** Universal AST, surface syntax, Rule R policy, stream protocol, FFI correlation, lease/custody arbitration, fencing, durable deduplication and integer/scalar encoding. Their tests may still need fixture or setup changes.\n\n   Stage E holder/authority logic should not be rewritten wholesale. Its integration tests must run against the new transport, especially crash cuts and successor publication.\n\n7. **Sequencing**\n\n   1. **Amend identity now**, before register implementation: stack §2/D9/D12, register invariant/identity sections, targets T-D12, UCF profiles, linker and emitter keys. Mark staged implementation honestly. This ruling does not depend on the new VM.\n   2. Freeze canonical numbering, structured grammar, ownership, window membership, call/return and tail-effect delivery, stamps, and rejection rules.\n   3. Build the new linearizer and shared validator. Gate on deterministic vectors, malformed-input refusal, and agreement between direct and datom load paths.\n   4. Build the semantic register evaluator beside the old evaluator. Gate on walker parity, Rule R, effects, recursion, tail calls and continuation invocation.\n   5. Build A-derived resolution and both lowerings. Gate on lexical-address agreement, behavioral parity, and the explicit H/R byte-identity decision.\n   6. Implement canonical UCF reconstruction and row transport. Gate on bidirectional safepoint tests, pending waits, nested/reified captures, module contexts, and refusal cases.\n   7. Run full Stage D/E handoff, fencing, durability, crash-cut and cross-process acceptance.\n   8. Delete the old semantic evaluator, rename the replacement, remove obsolete H/R request paths and `raise` dependencies, and finalize documentation.\n\n   Keep the old evaluator only as a temporary oracle. The final clean break needs no compatibility shim.\n\n8. **Risks and the honest case against**\n\n   The strongest case against is that **A-only identity does not require replacing the semantic VM**. It can be implemented around the current canonical vector. The additional foundation change should earn its cost through a clearer executable grammar and demonstrably simpler, trustworthy lowerings.\n\n   Potential mistakes include:\n\n   - Accepting arbitrary register vectors while relying on an expression-only inverse.\n   - Claiming no liveness while silently retaining dead values or deriving inconsistent windows.\n   - Making numbering depend on physical allocation or optimization.\n   - Losing tail-call, reified-continuation, or module-store semantics.\n   - Treating H/R provenance as optional or trusting an asserted A association without checking the deterministic derivation.\n   - Combining VM replacement, identity migration, row transport, and native-format changes without separate gates.\n\n   Stage E has just established difficult guarantees around pending effects, fencing, custody, durability, and crash recovery. Those mechanisms need not change conceptually, but changing their captured state invalidates integration evidence until rerun. This is substantial re-foundation cost, not a notation cleanup.\n\n   Proceed if the design restricts degrees of freedom and proves the lowerings. Reconsider implementation if stackification requires a new general storage machine or if preserving existing behavior demands extensive decompilation machinery.\n\nVERDICT: PROCEED_WITH_CHANGES — 1) amend A-only identity contracts; 2) freeze structured grammar and register minting; 3) specify windows and result delivery; 4) replace local-stack-expansion and allocator-reuse claims with explicit algorithms; 5) decide format versions from byte/contract evidence; 6) gate deletion on full Stage D/E regression.\n### 4.1 Configuration\n\n$$\\langle C, E, S, K \\rangle \\quad\\text{where}\\quad\nC = \\langle seg, pc, val, St \\rangle$$\n\n- **C (control)**: the segment id, the program counter, the accumulator\n  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\n  because it is part of what the machine is *doing*, and it is saved in\n  frames).\n- **E (environment)**: a persistent map `sym → value`, extended on closure\n  entry with `merge closure-env (engine/bind-params params args)`, as the\n  walker does. `bind-params` binds each param to its positional argument and\n  **nil-fills** any parameter left over on an under-arity call — it is not\n  `zipmap`, which would silently omit the unbound parameter names from the\n  extended environment entirely (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §7.7.2's\n  argument-to-named-parameter binding rule: arguments match `params` by\n  position, each bound to its name; under-arity → missing names bound to\n  `nil`; extra arguments beyond `params`' length are dropped). Lexical\n  addressing is reserved (§6.4).\n- **S (store)**: the map `key → value` holding `yin/def` results, stream\n  handles, cursor entries, and the FFI pair — unchanged from `engine`.\n- **K (continuation)**: a vector of frames, innermost last. Frame kinds:\n  `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the\n  effect-continuation frames the engine already defines\n  (`:dao.stream.apply/eval-call`, `:request-sent`), extended with\n  `:segment :pc :stack`.\n\nLet `I = code[seg][pc]` be the decoded instruction and `I.x` its operands.\n\n### 4.2 Transitions for the core instructions\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{const}:&& \\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,I.v,\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{var}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\rho(E,S,I.name),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{closure}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.entry,seg,E),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{push}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,val,\\,St \\Vert [val],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{jump}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,I.target,\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{branch-false}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,(val\\ ?\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{halt}:&& \\to \\text{halted},\\ \\text{result} = val\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $\\rho$ is `engine/resolve-var` (env → store → primitives → modules).\n\n**define** (Rule R: `yin/def` is syntax, never a name):\n\n$$\\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,val,\\,St,E,\\,S[I.name \\mapsto val],\\,K\\rangle$$\n\nThe value operand has already been evaluated into $val$; the write goes\nthrough `engine/store-put`, the one program store write, which refuses\nthe reserved key. The definition operator is never resolved: a\ndefinition lowers to its value's instructions followed by\n`[:define name]`, never to a call. $\\rho$ refuses `yin/def` before it\nconsults $E$ or $S$ (`:reserved-name`), and a well-formed segment has no\n`:var` naming it (S2.6 rule 8).\n\n**call** with $n = I.argc$, $St = St' \\Vert [f, a_1..a_n]$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ \\neg tail:&&\n\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,|St'|)]\\rangle\\\\\n&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ tail:&&\n\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a)\\ \\text{a value } v:&&\n\\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,v,\\,St',E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a)\\ \\text{an effect } \\epsilon:&&\n\\to \\mathrm{effect}(\\epsilon, \\langle seg,pc{+}1,St',E,S,K\\rangle)\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nA tail call grows neither $K$ nor $St$: the walker gets this for free\nbecause a closure body runs against the caller's $k$; the linear machine\nmust state it, and does. Effect handling `effect(ε, cfg)` is\n`engine/handle-effect`; its result is either a value (continue at `pc+1`)\nor a park (the configuration becomes a wait entry, control goes idle).\n\n**return** with $K = K' \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg',pc',E',b)]$:\n\n$$\\to \\langle seg',\\,pc',\\,val,\\,St[0..b),\\,E',\\,S,\\,K'\\rangle$$\n\nand with $K = []$: halted with result $val$.\n\n**stream-next** (cursor ref $c = val$), by outcome of `next` on the handle\nbehind $c$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&ok\\ v,\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,v,\\,St,E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n&blocked:&& \\to \\text{parked}\\ \\{seg,pc{+}1,E,St,K,\\ \\text{cursor-ref}\\ c\\}\\ \\in\\ \\text{wait-set}\\\\\n&end:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{nil},\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&gap\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\text{:dao.stream/gap},\\,St,E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],\\,K\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**ffi-call** ($St = St' \\Vert [a_1..a_n]$): park frame\n$\\{\\text{eval-call}, seg, pc{+}1, E, St', K\\}$ under a fresh id $p$; append\n$\\mathrm{request}(p, I.op, a)$ to call-in; on `ok` the wait set gains a\nreader entry on the call-out cursor correlated by $p$; when the correlated\nresponse arrives, $val \\leftarrow$ its `ok` value (or its `error` raises)\nand control resumes at $pc{+}1$.\n\n**park**: $K$, $E$, $St$, $seg$, $pc{+}1$ are written to `:parked` under a\nfresh id; the VM halts with that record as its value.\n**resume** ($val$ = resume value, $I.id$ the parked id): the parked\nconfiguration is restored with $val$ as its accumulator.\n**current-continuation**: $val \\leftarrow \\{seg, pc{+}1, E, St, K\\}$\nreified as data.\n\n### 4.3 What the equations make visible\n\n### 5.3 The flattening\n\nRecursive descent over the AST in evaluation order, emitting instructions\ninto a growing vector and lambda bodies out of line:\n\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| AST node                                                | Emitted sequence                                                   |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:application op args` (tail? τ)                        | `⟦op⟧ push ⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push call n τ`                         |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:if t c a`                                             | `⟦t⟧ branch-false →A ⟦c⟧ jump →E A: ⟦a⟧ E:`                        |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:dao.stream.apply/call op args`                        | `⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push ffi-call op n`                              |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/put target val`                                | `⟦target⟧ push ⟦val⟧ stream-put`                                   |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/cursor s`, `:stream/next s`, `:stream/close s` | `⟦s⟧ stream-cursor` etc.                                           |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/make b`                                        | `stream-make b`                                                    |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/store-get k`, `:vm/store-put k v`, `:vm/gensym p`  | one instruction each                                               |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation`                  | one instruction each                                               |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/resume id v`                                       | `⟦v⟧ resume id`                                                    |\n### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n\n`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\nelse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\nwith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\ndirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\nsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\ntype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\nown table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\nreturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\nfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n\n### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\nnot a boundary opcode. The lift maps it back to the definition\napplication. The validator's structural `reserved-rule` refuses a\n`:load-free`, `:define`, `:store-get`, or `:store-put` naming `yin/def`\n(`:reserved-name`), so the old `[:load-free rd yin/def]` call shape\nnever loads, and `resolve-var` refuses the name before env or store.\n\nRule R moved the register contract to `contract-version` 4, the \"r2\"\ncontract (`yin.vm/register-contract`). `load-image [vm segment contract]`\nand `create-vm` (`:contract` in its options) require that stamp and\nrefuse `:contract-missing` or `:contract-mismatch` before validation;\n`create-vm` also refuses a `:free-env`, `:store`, or `:primitives` that\nbinds `yin/def`. Every \"r1\" image is refused by stamp, with no\nmigration.\n\nEffect descriptors remain ordinary data passed to `yin.vm.engine`; no\ninstruction contains a callback, timer, handle, or scheduler. The `live`\noperands are the section 4.5 continuation boundary. They are present only\nwhere an instruction can suspend or reify the continuation. A normal\n`:call` already has one because a dynamically resolved primitive may return\nan effect descriptor.\n\n### 4.5 Live sets at continuation boundaries\n\nA non-tail `:call`, a blocking stream operation, an FFI call, `:park`, and\n`:current-continuation` can save a register body's state. Some registers\nare already dead at each boundary. A saved continuation carries only live\nregisters, never dead ones: retaining the whole file would retain dead\nvalues in long-running stream pipelines and actor loops as unaccounted\nstate. Liveness is computed at lowering time and recorded in the image,\nwhere it is visible, hashed, and verified.\n\nThe in-band `live` operand is the set of registers of the current body\nwhose values are read after the operation completes before being written\nagain. It excludes `rd`, which completion writes. It is inside R's\npreimage because the kernel discards everything else; a wrong live set\nchanges observable results. A side table would be outside wire\nverification. The named datoms remain the authority, and every receiver\nrecomputes `live` from the validated image before execution.\n\nRegister numbering: `live` names physical registers by the same indices\n`rd`, `fn-reg`, and `arg-regs` already use, whatever the body's numbering\nof its local and temporary banks is. It never introduces a second index\nspace. Frames captured for `:load-bound` and closures are not registers\nand are outside the live set; they are carried by the return frame as B3\ncarries them.\n\nThe computation is one pure function of the instruction vector and the\nbody ranges, `body-liveness`, exported by the register code namespace and\nused by both the lowerer, to fill the operand, and the validator, to check\nit. Per body:\n\n    use/def, from the section 4.4 table:\n      :const rd _              def {rd}\n      :load-bound rd _ _       def {rd}\n      :load-free rd _          def {rd}\n      :closure rd _ _          def {rd}\n      :store-get rd _          def {rd}\n      :store-put rd _ _        def {rd}\n      :gensym rd _             def {rd}\n      :stream-make rd _        def {rd}\n      :stream-put rd s v _     use {s v}           def {rd}\n      :stream-cursor rd s      use {s}             def {rd}\n      :stream-next rd c _      use {c}             def {rd}\n      :stream-close rd s       use {s}             def {rd}\n      :ffi-call rd _ args _    use args            def {rd}\n      :current-continuation rd _                   def {rd}\n      :park rd _                                  def {rd}\n      :resume _ v               use {v}\n      :move rd rs              use {rs}            def {rd}\n      :call rd f args tail? _  use {f} + args      def {rd} unless tail?\n      :branch-false c _        use {c}\n      :jump _                  none\n      :return r                use {r}\n      :halt r                  use {r}\n\n    successors within the body:\n      :jump t                  {t}\n      :branch-false c t        {t, pc + 1}\n      :return, :halt, :resume, tail :call   {}\n      every other instruction  {pc + 1}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '220,265p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '305,450p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '480,615p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '2565,2660p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '135,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '360,385p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '220,265p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '305,450p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '480,615p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '2565,2660p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '135,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '360,385p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   The request is retained, never dropped. A `full` call-in leaves the\n   identical encoded request in the polling wait set, which retries it, and\n   the call starts waiting for its response only once the append has\n   succeeded. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` fail the call\n   here and name their outcome.\"\n  [state op args k env]\n  (let [{:keys [call-in]} (ffi/require-call-pair! (:resources state) op)\n        ;; the request carries `args` out of the VM's view\n        state (engine/pin-refs state args)\n        response-cont {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call, :next k, :env env}\n        parked-id (ffi/call-id state (engine/park-id state))\n        parked (engine/park-continuation state\n                                         {:k response-cont, :env env}\n                                         parked-id)\n        request (apply2/request parked-id op (vec args))\n        result (ffi/put-request state call-in request)]\n    (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n      :dao.stream/ok\n      (-> parked\n          (update :wait-set\n                  (fnil conj [])\n                  (ffi/call-response-wait-entry parked-id k env))\n          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :bridge {:bridge-op op})\n          (assoc :control nil\n                 :k nil\n                 :value :yin/blocked\n                 :blocked? true\n                 :halted? false))\n      :dao.stream/full\n      (-> parked\n          (update :wait-set\n                  (fnil conj [])\n                  {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/request-sent,\n                       :parked-id parked-id,\n                       :next k,\n                       :env env,\n                       :op op},\n                   :env env,\n                   :reason :put,\n                   :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key,\n                   :datom request})\n          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :bridge {:bridge-op op})\n          (assoc :control nil\n                 :k nil\n                 :value :yin/blocked\n                 :blocked? true\n    (if (and (nil? node) k)\n      (let [cont-type (:type k)]\n        (case cont-type\n          :eval-operator\n          (let [frame (:frame k)\n                fn-value (:value state)\n                operands (:operands frame)\n                saved-env (or (:env k) env)]\n            (if (empty? operands)\n              (apply-function state fn-value [] (:next k) saved-env)\n              (let [updated-frame (assoc frame\n                                         :operator-evaluated? true\n                                         :fn fn-value)]\n                (cesk-return state\n                             (first operands)\n                             saved-env\n                             (assoc k\n                                    :type :eval-operand\n                                    :frame updated-frame)\n                             nil))))\n          :eval-operand\n          (let [frame (:frame k)\n                operand-value (:value state)\n                evaluated (conj (or (:evaluated frame) []) operand-value)\n                operands (:operands frame)\n                saved-env (or (:env k) env)]\n            (if (= (count evaluated) (count operands))\n              (apply-function state (:fn frame) evaluated (:next k) saved-env)\n              (let [next-idx (count evaluated)\n                    next-node (nth operands next-idx)\n                    updated-frame (assoc frame :evaluated evaluated)]\n                (cesk-return state\n                             next-node\n                             saved-env\n                             (assoc k :frame updated-frame)\n                             nil))))\n          :eval-test\n          (let [frame (:frame k)\n                test-value (:value state)\n                saved-env (or (:env k) env)\n                branch (if test-value (:consequent frame) (:alternate frame))]\n            (cesk-return state branch saved-env (:next k) test-value))\n          :dao.stream.apply/eval-operand\n          (let [frame (:frame k)\n                operand-value (:value state)\n                evaluated (conj (or (:evaluated frame) []) operand-value)\n                operands (:operands frame)\n                saved-env (or (:env k) env)]\n            (if (= (count evaluated) (count operands))\n              (park-and-call state (:op frame) evaluated (:next k) saved-env)\n              (let [next-idx (count evaluated)\n                    next-node (nth operands next-idx)\n                    updated-frame (assoc frame :evaluated evaluated)]\n                (cesk-return state\n                             next-node\n                             saved-env\n                             (assoc k :frame updated-frame)\n                             nil))))\n          :dao.stream.apply/request-sent\n          ;; A request retained on `full` has now been appended by the polling\n          ;; wait set. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n          ;; exactly as an immediately-sent one does.\n          (-> state\n              (update :wait-set\n                      (fnil conj [])\n                      (ffi/call-response-wait-entry (:parked-id k)\n                                                    (:next k)\n                                                    (:env k)))\n              (telemetry/emit-snapshot :bridge {:bridge-op (:op k)})\n              (assoc :control nil\n                     :k nil\n                     :value :yin/blocked\n                     :blocked? true\n                     :halted? false))\n          :dao.stream.apply/eval-call\n          ;; The response consumed here completes the call that\n          ;; `park-and-call` recorded, so its continuation leaves `:parked`\n          ;; rather than accumulating for the lifetime of the VM.\n          (let [state (update state :parked dissoc (:call-id k))]\n            (cesk-return state\n                         nil\n                         env\n                         (:next k)\n                         (ffi/call-result (:value state) (:call-id k))))\n          :eval-stream-put-target (let [frame (:frame k)\n                                        stream-ref (:value state)\n                                        val-node (:val frame)]\n                                    (cesk-return state\n                                                 val-node\n                                                 env\n                                                 (assoc k\n                                                        :type :eval-stream-put-val\n                                                        :stream-ref stream-ref)\n                                                 stream-ref))\n          :eval-stream-put-val\n          (let [val (:value state)\n                stream-ref (:stream-ref k)\n                effect (module/make-effect :stream/put {:stream stream-ref, :val val})\n                {:keys [state value blocked?]}\n                (engine/handle-effect state\n                                      effect\n                                      {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\n                                       :park-entry-fns\n                                       {:stream/put (fn [_s _e r]\n                                                      {:k (:next k),\n                                                       :env env,\n                                                       :reason :put,\n                                                       :stream-id\n                                                       (:stream-id r),\n                                                       :datom val})}})]\n            (if blocked?\n              (assoc state\n                     :control nil\n                     :k nil\n                     :halted? false)\n              (cesk-return state nil env (:next k) value)))\n          :eval-stream-close-source\n          (let [stream-ref (:value state)\n                effect (module/make-effect :stream/close {:stream stream-ref})\n                {:keys [state value]} (engine/handle-effect\n                                        state\n                                        effect\n                                        {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore})]\n            (cesk-return state nil env (:next k) value))\n          :eval-stream-cursor-source\n          (let [stream-ref (:value state)\n                effect (module/make-effect :stream/cursor {:stream stream-ref})\n                {:keys [state value]} (engine/handle-effect\n                                        state\n                                        effect\n                                        {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore})]\n            (cesk-return state nil env (:next k) value))\n          :eval-stream-next-cursor\n          (let [cursor-ref (:value state)\n                effect (module/make-effect :stream/next {:cursor cursor-ref})\n                {:keys [state value blocked?]}\n                (engine/handle-effect\n                  state\n                  effect\n                  {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\n                   :park-entry-fns {:stream/next\n                                    (fn [_s _e r]\n                                      {:k (:next k),\n                                       :env env,\n                                       :reason :next,\n                                       :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref r),\n        :variable\n        (let [value (engine/resolve-var env\n                                        (engine/active-store\n                                          state (engine/env-store-of env))\n                                        primitives modules (:name node))]\n          (cesk-return state nil env k value))\n        :lambda (let [{:keys [params body]} node]\n                  (cesk-return\n                    state\n                    nil\n                    env\n                    k\n                    (closure-of (:owner state) node params body env)))\n        :application\n        (if (vm/definition? node)\n          ;; Rule R: a definition never resolves its operator. The key is\n          ;; the literal first operand; only the value operand runs.\n          (cesk-return state\n                       (second (:operands node))\n                       env\n                       {:type :eval-define,\n                        :name (vm/definition-name node),\n                        :next k,\n                        :env env}\n                       (:value state))\n          (cesk-return state\n                       (:operator node)\n                       env\n                       {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-operator}\n                       (:value state)))\n        :if (cesk-return state\n                         (:test node)\n                         env\n                         {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-test}\n                         (:value state))\n        :dao.stream.apply/call\n        (let [operands (or (:operands node) [])\n              op (:op node)]\n          (if (empty? operands)\n            (park-and-call state op [] k env)\n            (cesk-return state\n                         (first operands)\n                         env\n                         {:frame {:op op, :operands operands, :evaluated []},\n                          :next k,\n                          :env env,\n                          :type :dao.stream.apply/eval-operand}\n                         (:value state))))\n        :vm/gensym (let [prefix (or (:prefix node) \"id\")\n                         [id s'] (engine/gensym state prefix)]\n                     (cesk-return s' nil env k id))\n        ;; A module closure's store nodes route to its module store, with\n        ;; no ambient fallback (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r7).\n        :vm/store-get (do (engine/check-store-key! (:key node))\n                          (cesk-return state nil env k\n                                       (get (engine/active-store\n                                              state\n                                              (engine/env-store-of env))\n                                            (:key node))))\n        :vm/store-put (let [key (:key node)\n                            value (:val node)]\n                        (cesk-return (engine/put-active\n                                       state (engine/env-store-of env)\n                                       key value)\n                                     nil env k value))\n        :vm/store-update (let [key (:key node)\n                               f (:fn node)\n                               args (:args node)\n                               store-of (engine/env-store-of env)\n                               _ (engine/check-store-key! key)\n                               current (get (engine/active-store state store-of)\n                                            key)\n                               new-value (apply f current args)]\n                           (cesk-return (engine/put-active state store-of key\n                                                           new-value)\n                                        nil env k new-value))\n        :vm/current-continuation\n        (cesk-return state\n                     nil\n                     env\n                     k\n                     (values/continuation (:owner state)\n                                          {:type :reified-continuation,\n                                           :k k,\n                                           :env env}))\n        :vm/park (-> (engine/park-continuation state {:k k, :env env})\n                     (assoc :control nil\n                            :k nil))\n        :vm/resume (cesk-return state\n                                (:val node)\n                                env\n                                {:type :eval-resume-val,\n                                 :parked-id (:parked-id node),\n                                 :next k,\n                                 :env env}\n                                (:value state))\n        :stream/make (let [capacity (or (:buffer node)\n                                        vm/default-stream-capacity)\n                           effect (module/make-effect :stream/make {:capacity capacity})\n                           {:keys [state value]} (engine/handle-effect\n                                                   state\n                                                   effect\n                                                   {:restore-fn\n                                                    ast-walker-restore})]\n                       (cesk-return state nil env k value))\n        :stream/put\n        (cesk-return\n          state\n          (:target node)\n          env\n          {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-put-target}\n          (:value state))\n        :stream/cursor\n        (cesk-return\n          state\n          (:source node)\n          env\n          {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-cursor-source}\n          (:value state))\n        :stream/next\n        (cesk-return\n          state\n          (:source node)\n          env\n          {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-next-cursor}\n          (:value state))\n        :stream/close\n        (cesk-return\n          state\n          (:source node)\n          env\n          {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-close-source}\n          (:value state))\n        (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type\" {:type type, :node node}))))))\n\n\n(defn handle-effect\n  \"Dispatch an effect and return {:state updated-state :value v :blocked? bool}.\n   park-entry-fns maps :stream/put and :stream/next to functions that build\n   wait entries.\n\n   `effect` must be a `yin.vm.effect` value minted by `module/make-effect`\n   (D4); a map, whatever its keys, is data and is refused here.\n\n   A parked entry is stored as the builder left it plus, for a writer, the\n   `:datom` it retries: registers and resource ids only. Neither the live\n   stream handle nor a restore closure is attached — `check-wait-set`\n   resolves handles from `:resources` at every poll, and the scheduler that pops\n   a woken entry dispatches restoration itself — so a blocked entry is pure\n   data and survives serialization.\"\n  [state effect {:keys [park-entry-fns], :as opts}]\n  (when-not (module/effect? effect)\n    (fail \"Not an effect\" {:reason :not-an-effect}))\n  (let [park-entry (get park-entry-fns (:effect effect))\n        result\n        (case (:effect effect)\n          :vm/store-put {:state (put-active state\n                                            (store-context state)\n                                            (:key effect)\n                                            (:val effect)),\n                         :value (:val effect),\n                         :blocked? false}\n          :stream/make\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym state \"stream\")\n                [stream-ref new-state] (handle-make s' effect id)]\n            {:state new-state, :value stream-ref, :blocked? false})\n          :stream/cursor\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym state \"cursor\")\n                [cursor-ref new-state]\n                (case (gate-refuse! state effect)\n                  :running\n                  (handle-cursor-unminted s' effect id (or (:id-counter state) 0))\n                  (handle-cursor s' effect id))]\n            {:state new-state, :value cursor-ref, :blocked? false})\n          :stream/put\n          (let [state (pin-refs state (:val effect))\n                result (handle-put state effect)]\n            (if (:park result)\n              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\n                                  (park-entry state effect result))\n                    built-entry (if (and built-entry (not (:datom built-entry)))\n                                  (assoc built-entry :datom (:val effect))\n                                  built-entry)]\n                (if (and built-entry (= :running (vm/gate-mode state)))\n                  (let [[n s'] (issue-number (:state result))]\n                    (handle-stream-block\n                      (assoc result :state s')\n                      (assoc built-entry :yin.k/issue n)))\n                  (handle-stream-block result built-entry)))\n              {:state (:state result),\n               :value (:value result),\n               :blocked? false}))\n          :stream/next\n          (let [result (handle-next state effect)]\n            (if (:park result)\n              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\n                                  (park-entry state effect result))]\n                (handle-stream-block result built-entry))\n              {:state (:state result),\n               :value (:value result),\n               :blocked? false}))\n          :stream/poll\n          (let [result (handle-poll state effect)]\n            (if (and (:park result) (= :running (vm/gate-mode state)))\n              (let [builder (or (get park-entry-fns :stream/poll)\n                                (get park-entry-fns :stream/next))\n                    built (when builder (builder state effect result))]\n                (handle-stream-block\n                  result\n                  (when built\n                    (assoc built\n                           :reason :observe\n                           :op :poll\n                           :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\n                           :stream-id (:stream-id result)))))\n              {:state (:state result),\n               :value (:value result),\n               :blocked? false}))\n          :stream/close\n          (let [_ (gate-refuse! state effect)\n                close-result (handle-close state effect)]\n            {:state (:state close-result), :value nil, :blocked? false})\n          ;; `:heap` is VM state, not continuation state: a write is seen\n          ;; by every holder of the ref and survives continuation invocation\n          :cell/new\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym (count-allocation state (:val effect)) \"cell\")\n                ref (issue-ref s' :cell-ref id)]\n            {:state (heap-write s' id (:seal ref) (:val effect)),\n             :value ref,\n             :blocked? false})\n          :cell/get\n          (let [id (check-ref! state :cell/get :cell-ref (:cell effect))]\n(defn semantic-restore\n  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\n\n   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n\n   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n     response reader and the machine stays blocked. A writer carrying a\n     lowered call's response route (`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`)\n     waits on that carried route; an ordinary one waits on the fixed local\n     call-out keys.\n   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n  ([base entry val]\n   (if (:request-sent entry)\n     (let [wait (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n                                     (:pc entry)\n                                     (:env entry)\n                                     (:stack entry)\n                                     (:k entry)\n                                     (:call-id entry))\n           wait (if-let [[cursor-key stream-key]\n                         (carried-response-route entry)]\n                  (assoc wait\n                         :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cursor-key}\n                         :stream-id stream-key)\n                  wait)]\n       (assoc base\n              :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) [])) wait)\n              :control nil\n              :k nil\n              :value :yin/blocked\n              :blocked? true\n              :halted? false))\n     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n       (put-registers base\n                      (:segment entry)\n                      (:pc entry)\n                      val\n                      (:stack entry)\n                      (:env entry)\n                      (:k entry))))))\n\n\n(defn- call-park-entries\n  \"Wait-entry builders for an instruction whose effect may block. The\n(defn lower-rows\n  \"Lower one projected row set to the canonical instruction vector of UCF\n   §7.3.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.1): one positional\n   tuple per instruction, pc is the index, defaults saturated, refs\n   resolved to pcs, no header. `[:call argc tail?]` is kept unfolded for\n   the decoder to fold into `:tailcall`, exactly as the datom lane's\n   `load-image` does; a lowering that emitted the folded form would hash a\n   different vector and break the projection-path equality of U5.\n\n   `bc` is `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`'s `{:root id, :rows {id row}}`;\n   it is validated (§7.4) first and a defect throws naming it. `origin` is\n   nil for the legacy bare-row lane or one of §2.5's ruled portable shapes;\n   arbitrary opaque values are rejected. Returns `{:vector v, :provenance [[pc origin root\n   path] …]}` with one provenance row per pc in pc order, `path` being the\n   emitting node's §2.5 structural path (row-position steps — id 0, tag 1,\n   first slot 2; a `nodes` item a `[position i]` pair) rooted at the\n   tree's root row id, so every provenance path joins `occurrences`'s\n   relation on `[root path]` (§5.3).\"\n  ([bc] (lower-rows bc nil))\n  ([{:keys [root rows] :as bc} origin]\n   (when-not (or (nil? origin) (vm/occurrence-origin? origin))\n     (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower rows with a malformed occurrence origin\"\n                     {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\n   (when-let [{:keys [rule path id]} (vm/validate-rows bc)]\n     (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower rows that fail validation\"\n                     (cond-> {:rule rule}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '170,215p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '290,330p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '555,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '590,665p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; head -65 src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc; head -24 src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '170,215p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '290,330p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '555,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '590,665p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; head -65 src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc; head -24 src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:call`                 | `:yin.code/argc n`, `:yin.code/tail? bool`    | pop `n` args and the operator below them; apply (§4.2)                           |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:return`               | —                                             | pop frame from `K`; restore `pc`, `E`, stack base; `val` unchanged; empty `K`    |\n|                         |                                               | halts                                                                            |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:jump`                 | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← target`                                                                    |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:branch-false`         | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← val ? pc+1 : target`                                                       |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:halt`                 | —                                             | halt with `val` as the result                                                    |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:gensym`               | `:yin.code/prefix`                            | `val ← fresh id`; id counter advances                                            |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:store-get`            | `:yin.code/key`                               | `val ← S[key]`                                                                   |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:store-put`            | `:yin.code/key`, `:yin.code/value`            | `S[key] ← v; val ← v`                                                            |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:define`               | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `S[name] <- val`; `val` unchanged; the operator is never resolved (Rule R)       |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream-make`          | `:yin.code/buffer`                            | effect `:stream/make` via engine                                                 |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream-put`           | —                                             | target ref popped from `St`, value in `val`; effect `:stream/put`                |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream-cursor`        | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/cursor`                                     |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream-next`          | —                                             | cursor ref in `val`; effect `:stream/next`                                       |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream-close`         | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/close`                                      |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:park`                 | —                                             | `engine/park-continuation` with the current frame                                |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:resume`               | `:yin.code/parked-id`                         | resume value in `val`; `engine/resume-continuation`                              |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:current-continuation` | —                                             | `val ← {:type :reified-continuation :segment :pc :env :stack :k}`                |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:ffi-call`             | `:yin.code/ffi-op kw`, `:yin.code/argc n`     | pop `n` args; park-and-call over the FFI pair                                    |\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nThis is the AST vocabulary of `yin.vm/ast->datoms` made linear, one for\none, plus the three sequencing primitives (`:push`, `:jump`, `:branch-false`)\nand `:halt`. `:yin/macro-expand` and `:vm/store-update` are outside both\nevaluators' supported corpus and are rejected at lowering with an error\n   carries a §2.4 mnemonic (`:yin.code/op`); the later rules read both, so a\n   batch that lacks them cannot be judged by them (`:instruction-shape`).\n3. Instruction pcs are exactly `0 .. length-1`, each once.\n4. The batch is sorted by pc (so a loader can fill the array in one pass).\n5. Every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` resolves to an instruction\n   of the same segment, and a `:jump`, `:branch-false`, or `:closure`\n   without its required ref resolves nowhere and fails the same rule.\n6. Every basic block ends in a terminator (`:jump`, `:return`, `:halt`) or\n   falls into a labelled successor; pc `length-1` is a terminator.\n7. Every `:call`/`:ffi-call` has a non-negative `:yin.code/argc`.\n8. Rule R (`:reserved-name`): no `:var` names `yin/def`, no `:closure`\n   binds it, no `:store-get`/`:store-put` key names it, and every\n   `:define` names a symbol other than `yin/def`. A lowered image with\n   the old call shape `[:var yin/def] ... [:call 2 false]` never loads.\n\nViolation is a load error naming the entity and rule. The loader is total\nover the outcomes of its inputs; it does not guess. `yin.vm.code/rules`\nruns these eight, in this order, each assuming the earlier ones held; the\nfirst defect wins. Every persistent-code loader (`vm-load-program`,\n`load-vector`) takes a required contract and refuses `:contract-missing`\nor `:contract-mismatch` before these rules run.\n\n### 2.7 A worked segment\n\n`((fn [x] (+ x 1)) 10)` lowers to (pcs shown, refs written as `→pc`):\n\n```\nseg   :yin.code/type :segment   :yin.code/length 14\n0  :closure   params [x]  body →6\n1  :push\n2  :const     10\n3  :push\n4  :call      argc 1  tail? false\n5  :halt\n6  :var       +               ; body of the lambda, out of line\n7  :push\n8  :var       x\n9  :push\n10 :const     1\n11 :push\n12 :call      argc 2  tail? true\nframes, register pairs or continuation chains. Every declared cell must\nbe reached and every reached cell declared. Apply the same rule per\nchild. Pin roots needed by outstanding FFI/stream obligations according\nto the existing heap/resource rules; serialization is not reclamation.\n\n## 7. Eligibility and safepoint validation\n\n### 7.1 Whole-task requirements\n\nOnly blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine\nexport boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth\nkind. All ready queues are empty, all installs complete in the UCF sense,\nand root/child state is stable. Refuse any root or child with an observe\nwait, unapplied held observation, reachable unminted cursor, pending close,\nor link request without its installed response cursor. These are D4-D9\nholds, not occasions to invent a new pending variant or opcode.\n\nA successful lift is checked by the version-aware checkpoint inspector\nand complete restoration-grammar validator before it answers OK. A valid\ncode image alone proves neither a valid saved continuation nor a valid\ntask safepoint. Safepoint tables remain derived data, not stored flags.\n\n### 7.2 PC profiles\n\nFor semantic, derive safepoints with the existing ucf/safepoints semantics.\nFor stack, the site is resume PC minus one, in the captured layout; the\nsame opcode-to-reason table below applies. For register, the carried\nsite-pc and PC satisfy section 5.3 and the existing r2 validator.\n\n| Site opcode | Allowed pending reasons |\n| --- | --- |\n| `:stream-next` | next |\n| `:stream-put` | put |\n| `:ffi-call` | ffi, ffi-request |\n| `:call` | next, put, ffi, ffi-request, link-request, link-response, install |\n| `:park` | none; explicit parked record only |\n| `:current-continuation` | none; captured-value record only |\n\nReason names in this table are the corresponding unqualified keywords.\nCall permits dynamic effects; the pending must independently prove the\nactual effect and all correlation/resource constraints. Do not infer an\nFFI request merely from a call opcode. Parked-table entries in PC profiles\nmust name explicit park sites. FFI bookkeeping parked records are handled\nby 7.3, not mislabeled as explicit parks. A captured-value R must name a\ncurrent-continuation site. Return-frame sites are non-tail calls.\n\nFor stack, the saved resume PC and every return PC are strictly inside\nthe captured code-space length. An admitted b2 image ends at a jump,\nreturn or halt, not a suspending instruction; no valid park needs an\nout-of-range resume fetch. The general row-at one-past rule does not\nwaive this eligibility check. Register's inclusive body ranges and\n| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | St at park | the resume value |\n| blocked read | `:stream-next` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | St (cursor ref was in `val`) | the read value; `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n| blocked write | `:stream-put` → `full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped target ref | the written value, on retry `ok` |\n| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/ok` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | the correlated response's `ok` value; its `error` raises |\n| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | as sent, once the request is appended and the response correlates |\n| effectful call | `:call`/`:tailcall` whose operator yields a blocking effect (`require`, stream-module functions, effect handlers) | `pc+1` | St minus the popped operator and args | the effect's resume value, per its kind as above |\n| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` travels instead |\n\nTwo corrections to the first draft's table stand beside the addition.\n`:current-continuation` is **not** a safepoint: it does not park — it\nreifies `val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K}` and continues, so a reified\ncontinuation is a *value class* of the encoding (§7.5.1), never a lift\npoint. And `pc+1` as the resume pc is verified sound for every row above:\n§2.6 requires the final pc to be a terminator, so every listed instruction\nhas a successor in its own segment.\n\nThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\nencodings (§7.5):\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k/frame\n{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\n :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi\n                                          ; | :ffi-request | :call-effect\n                                          ; | :link-request | :link-response\n                                          ; | :install\n :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\n :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\n :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\n :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\n                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\n                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\n```\n\n**Linker safepoints (M4).** Every block marked (linker M4) is specified by\n`yin.vm.linker.md`; the private resources table, sealed references and\ninstall child were implemented in M4 (S3 ships r8-r11, the install child\nand link-module; S4 ships manifests). The running VM implements the\nsafepoint and resource semantics; only the UCF lift and lower of parked\nlink and install entries stays future. The require flow adds three\nsafepoints to the set above, one per wait state of a `:module/require`\neffect that misses the module registry (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2, 7.3); a\nhit answers without parking. The frame is the effectful-call row's in all\nthree -- `require` is named there -- and the wait entry moves between the\nthree reasons without the machine advancing, so the canonical resume pc\nand the stack are that row's in every state:\n\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n| Safepoint kind | Raised by                              | Resume pc | Stack in frame (St)    | Accumulator on resume                            |\n+================+========================================+===========+========================+==================================================+\n| :link-request  | the :module/require effect on a        | pc+1      | St minus the popped    | no resume from this state: on ok                 |\n|                | registry miss, before the link         |           | operator and args      | the entry becomes :link-response                 |\n|                | request envelope is appended ok;       |           |                        |                                                  |\n|                | a full retry keeps this state          |           |                        |                                                  |\n|                | (the :ffi-request discipline)          |           |                        |                                                  |\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n| :link-response | the request append's ok; the kept      | pc+1      | St minus the popped    | a matching refusal, or a failed                  |\n|                | cursor polls the link response         |           | operator and args      | discharge, raises as the effect's                |\n|                | stream for the correlated id           |           |                        | error                                            |\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n| :install       | a matching ok response, its            | pc+1      | St minus the popped    | the required module's symbol on                  |\n|                | obligations discharged, while          |           | operator and args      | linked; the refusal as the                       |\n|                | the install child runs its             |           |                        | effect's error on refused                        |\n|                | phases                                 |           |                        |                                                  |\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n\nThe pending variants of the three reasons are in section 7.4.3.\n\n**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\n(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\nempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\nconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n\n(ns yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile\n  \"R1 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md S4): `lower-register`\n   consumes `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`'s output (resolved tuples\n   plus binder side table) and emits a `:yin.debruijn.register/*` image\n   (`yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`'s `{:bodies [...], :instructions\n   [...]}` shape) plus a pc-keyed diagnostic side table. `lift` is the\n   reverse morphism, register image plus side table back to the same\n   named `:yin.code/*`-shaped canonical vector `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/\n   lift` produces for a stack image (design section 3: 'both lifts land\n   on the same named shape').\n\n   THE LOWERER: a target-register-passing walk, not a stack-machine\n   emit-then-move walk -- every node is lowered with a caller-supplied\n   destination register and emits instructions that end with its value\n   written there directly, so no `:move` is ever needed to relocate a\n   value after the fact (`:if`'s two arms both target the expression's\n   own one destination register for the same reason, per design section\n   4.3). A body's temporary bank (T0..) is allocated by a deterministic\n   linear-scan discipline: lowest free index first (`allocate-temp!`),\n   freed the moment a value is consumed by its parent (`free-temp!`), so\n   the physical count needed is exactly the peak number of temporaries\n   simultaneously live. Locals (L0..L(n-1)) are a disjoint reserved\n   range this phase's lowerer never targets or reads directly (`:load-\n   bound` always reads the runtime frame chain into a fresh temporary,\n   unchanged from B3's convention); see `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`'s\n   own docstring for why that is correct, not an oversight. Every\n   `:call`'s `live` operand (design section 4.5) is a placeholder `[]`\n   at emission time -- `fill-live` overwrites it in a second, separate\n   pass, per body, once every body's instructions and pc numbering are\n   final, calling `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/body-liveness` (a\n   backward dataflow over the emitted body, unrelated to the allocator's\n   own forward free-list state).\n\n   THE LIFT: the register image, viewed per body, is a flat instruction\n   sequence with a recoverable grammar -- an atomic producer (`:const`/\n   `:load-bound`/`:load-free`/`:closure`) is a complete expression by\n   itself; any other instruction starts a chain of one or more recursively\n   parsed sub-expressions terminated by exactly one `:call` (the chain is\n   `[fn, arg...]`) or exactly one `:branch-false` (the chain is `[test]`,\n   followed by the consequent and alternate sub-expressions in turn). This\n   holds because the lowerer above only ever emits instructions in that\n   shape (operator then operands left to right, fully expanded before the\n   next; `if` test, then one arm at a time). `parse-expr` is that\n   recursive-descent parser, translating each parsed production straight\n   into `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/named-canonical-vector`'s own tuple\n   shapes (`[:const v]`, `[:var name]`, `[:closure params body-pc]`,\n   `[:push]`, `[:call argc tail?]`, `[:branch-false target]`, `[:jump\n   target]`, `[:return]`, `[:halt]`) -- it does not need to consult\n   register identities at all, since the grammar alone (not register\n   numbers) determines every sub-expression's boundary; it threads an\n   explicit `base` (the absolute position the parsed production's own\n   output will occupy in its body's final translated vector) top-down,\n   so a `:branch-false`/`:jump` target is computed directly as an\n   absolute position, with no separate label-resolution pass needed on\n   this side (unlike the lowerer's own local labels, which exist only\n   because a lowered `:if`'s jump targets are not yet known when the\n   branch-false is first emitted -- here, both arms are fully parsed,\n   and so fully sized, before their surrounding tuple is assembled).\n   `:load-bound`/`:load-free` translate to `[:var name]` under the same\n   trust rule `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lift` uses (round-trip through\n   `resolve-name` before trusting a supplied side table's parameter\n   names).\n\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`, `yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `yin.vm.debruijn-\n   resolve`, and `yin.vm.debruijn.stack` are only read from, never\n(ns yin.vm.linearize\n  \"Lowering: Universal AST datoms (`:yin/*`) → linear executable datoms\n   (`:yin.code/*`), per `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.\n\n   Lowering is VM lineage and knows no surface syntax. It is a recursive\n   descent in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to\n   right), emitting the main sequence first and lambda bodies out of line\n   after it. Labels are symbolic while emitting and are resolved to\n   instruction refs in a second pass: the output carries refs, never pc\n   integers.\n\n   Lowering reads the AST datom index directly rather than reconstructing\n   maps, because every instruction names the AST entity it came from in\n   `:yin.code/source` and reconstructed maps have no entities.\n\n   The row lane (`lower-rows`, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`\n   §5.1/§5.3) lowers the projected row set to the canonical positional\n   instruction vector of UCF §7.3.2 instead, threading §2.5 structural\n   paths for the provenance side table. It shares this flattening order;\n   the datom lane stays beside it for media that carry datoms (§7.1's\n   projection path), and `rows-loader` composes the row lane for the row\n   medium an encoder observer forwards to (`yin.vm.encoder`).\"\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The draft addresses the earlier direction changes, but I found freeze blockers in the detailed rules. `L(pc+1)` normally includes the result destination, so the saved-window rule needs explicit subtraction. The grammar also omits scalar operations and rejects some nested conditionals that the prose permits. Tail-effect transport needs a closed state shape for validation and resume."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '135,184p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '224,268p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '2226,2258p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '350,375p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc; sed -n '470,510p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '365,405p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md | sed -n '470,545p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '135,184p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '224,268p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '2226,2258p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '350,375p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc; sed -n '470,510p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '365,405p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md | sed -n '470,545p'\"","aggregated_output":"(defn semantic-restore\n  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\n\n   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n\n   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n     response reader and the machine stays blocked. A writer carrying a\n     lowered call's response route (`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`)\n     waits on that carried route; an ordinary one waits on the fixed local\n     call-out keys.\n   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n  ([base entry val]\n   (if (:request-sent entry)\n     (let [wait (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n                                     (:pc entry)\n                                     (:env entry)\n                                     (:stack entry)\n                                     (:k entry)\n                                     (:call-id entry))\n           wait (if-let [[cursor-key stream-key]\n                         (carried-response-route entry)]\n                  (assoc wait\n                         :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cursor-key}\n                         :stream-id stream-key)\n                  wait)]\n       (assoc base\n              :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) [])) wait)\n              :control nil\n              :k nil\n              :value :yin/blocked\n              :blocked? true\n              :halted? false))\n     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n       (put-registers base\n                      (:segment entry)\n                      (:pc entry)\n                      val\n                      (:stack entry)\n                      (:env entry)\n                      (:k entry))))))\n\n\n(defn- call-park-entries\n(defn- apply-call\n  \"The §4.2 call transition, with the arguments already isolated from the\n   operand stack. Returns `{:goto [seg pc val St E K vm image]}` to continue,\n   `{:stop vm}` when the call blocked, or throws for a non-function.\n\n   A tail call grows neither K nor St: the frame the caller would have pushed\n   is the frame its own caller gave it. A primitive that yields an effect\n   parks with the continuation after the call site.\"\n  [code seg pc val St E K vm tail? f args]\n  (case (engine/operator-kind vm f)\n    :closure\n    (let [c (values/payload f)\n          E' (merge (:env c) (engine/bind-params (:params c) args))\n          frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\n                 :stack-base (count St)}\n          seg' (:segment c)]\n      {:goto [seg' (:entry c) val St E' (if tail? K (conj K frame))\n              vm (get code seg')]})\n    :host-fn\n    (let [result (apply f args)]\n      (if (module/effect? result)\n        (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n              (engine/handle-effect\n                (put-registers (engine/check-callee-effect! vm f result)\n                               seg pc val St E K)\n                result\n                {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\n          (if blocked?\n            {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n            {:goto [seg (inc pc) value St E K state (get code seg)]}))\n        {:goto [seg (inc pc) result St E K vm (get code seg)]}))\n    ;; An abortive jump: the call site's St/E/K are dropped and the\n    ;; captured registers take over, with the argument in val as the\n    ;; capture instruction left it (19). The store is not a register.\n    :continuation\n    (let [v (engine/continuation-argument args)\n          c (values/payload f)\n          seg' (:segment c)]\n      {:goto [seg' (:pc c) v (:stack c) (:env c) (:k c) vm\n              (get code seg')]})))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The hot loop\n;; =============================================================================\n   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n\n   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n\n   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n    (when (seq run-queue)\n      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n            ;; (cursor ids)\n            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n            base (assoc state\n                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n                        :blocked? false\n                        :halted? false)]\n        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n\n\n      ;; the lambda, as the datom lane's `(emit! lambda :return)` does.\n      (loop [i 0]\n        (when-let [[l body path] (get @bodies i)]\n          (mark! l)\n          (lower-node body (conj path 3))\n          (emit! path [:return])\n          (recur (inc i))))\n      [@code @labels @paths])))\n\n\n(defn lower-rows\n  \"Lower one projected row set to the canonical instruction vector of UCF\n   §7.3.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.1): one positional\n   tuple per instruction, pc is the index, defaults saturated, refs\n   resolved to pcs, no header. `[:call argc tail?]` is kept unfolded for\n   the decoder to fold into `:tailcall`, exactly as the datom lane's\n   `load-image` does; a lowering that emitted the folded form would hash a\n   different vector and break the projection-path equality of U5.\n\n   `bc` is `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`'s `{:root id, :rows {id row}}`;\n   it is validated (§7.4) first and a defect throws naming it. `origin` is\n   nil for the legacy bare-row lane or one of §2.5's ruled portable shapes;\n   arbitrary opaque values are rejected. Returns `{:vector v, :provenance [[pc origin root\n   path] …]}` with one provenance row per pc in pc order, `path` being the\n   emitting node's §2.5 structural path (row-position steps — id 0, tag 1,\n   first slot 2; a `nodes` item a `[position i]` pair) rooted at the\n`{:type :dao.stream.apply/call :op op :operands operands}` AST; project it\nthrough the v3 row codec. Lower rebuilds the native three-field frame.\nThe source provenance need not be guessed from an equal call elsewhere:\nrow content, not AST object identity, determines its address.\n\nOther node-bearing arms lower the addressed node as their native `:frame`.\nExtra AST metadata never travels. Source-position metadata is not state.\nEval-define and eval-resume-val encode their native scalar fields directly;\nthese frames legitimately do not retain an original AST node. Lower may\nnot invent one. All dynamic fields, including saved environments and\npartially evaluated operands, are census roots.\n\nA Kw chain is finite; literal guest values in it may contain further\nencoded continuations, but structural host cycles are non-portable.\nUnknown frame types, missing operands or opaque map-AST fallback refuse.\nRestore calls the native walker return transition with control nil,\ndecoded env/k and the delivered value, without evaluating any AST node\nuntil the scheduler runs after admission. No artificial PC is introduced.\n\n## 6. Values, closures and dependency census\n\nUse the disjoint UCF 7.5 value grammar. In particular, every guest map\nis a `:yin.k/literal` marker with encoded key/value entries. Structural\nregister maps, AST rows and pending maps are not guest literal maps.\nScalars/collections retain the canonical Jing carrier class. Resource\nreferences are authenticated at lift and reminted under the receiver's\nowner at lower. No source capability secret travels.\n\nClosure markers use the existing kernel module seams, now validated\nunder the enclosing profile:\n\n| Profile | Marker fields beyond `:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure` |\n| --- | --- |\n| semantic | binding `:named`, format `:yin.semantic/code`, segment A, entry local PC, params, env bindings, optional store-of |\n| stack | binding `:positional`, format `:yin.debruijn.code`, segment H, entry image-relative PC, arity, frames F, optional store-of |\n| register | binding `:positional`, format `:yin.debruijn.register`, segment R, entry image-relative PC, arity, frames F, optional store-of |\n| walker | binding `:named`, format `:yin.ast/code`, segment lambda-row A, entry nil, params, env bindings, optional store-of |\n\nAll field names above carry the existing `:yin.k/` namespace. Named\nclosure env is the existing `{S V}` bindings map, not E; its store-of is\nthe sibling marker key. Positional entry points are image-relative even\nrefuses on its diagnostic, identically to `lower-stack`, before emitting\nanything. It does not accept named datoms directly, does not rerun name\nresolution, and does not accept a stack image. B2 remains the only\nproducer of `:yin.debruijn.code/*` stack images; this lowerer is the only\nproducer of `:yin.debruijn.register/*` register images.\n\nBecause the input is an expression graph rather than a flat instruction\nstream, there is no control-flow graph to recover and no abstract operand\nstack to simulate: every intermediate value is an expression whose extent\nis known from its record. The walk is the named linearizer's order,\noperator then operands left to right, lambda bodies out of line in\ndiscovery order, `if` evaluating one arm, with occurrences expanded\npositionally: a resolved record referenced twice is lowered twice, at two\nplaces, exactly as `lower-stack` and `lower` do. Every body has one\nenclosing lexical chain by construction, and every bound reference is\nalready validated against it. The lowerer's own checks are register bounds,\nbody ranges, and target bounds, applied to its output.\n\n### 4.2 Register classes\n\nEach body has two disjoint register banks:\n\n    L0 ... L(n-1)     current body's parameters\n    T0 ... T(k-1)     expression temporaries\n\n`n` is the body's declared arity. `:load-bound rd depth position` reads the\nexisting lexical frame chain and writes `rd`; it does not assign an outer\nframe's slot a register number in the current body. Thus frame 0 remains the\ninnermost frame and the runtime frame vector remains outermost-first, exactly\nas in B3. `:load-free rd name` preserves the existing resolution order.\n\nThe allocator gives every intermediate expression value a virtual temporary\nin evaluation order, then performs deterministic linear-scan allocation\nwithin each body. Locals are never coalesced with temporaries. The physical\nregister count is the number of local registers plus the maximum\nsimultaneously live temporary registers. If a target requires a bounded\nregister file, deterministic spill slots are allocated after the last\nregister using the same order; spilling is a later kernel concern and is not\nimplicit in R1.\n\n### 4.3 Determinism\n   470\ttwo load paths, projection to datoms with eid `(inc pc)`) stands with\n   471\tthe new table substituted. A is the only code identity; H and R are\n   472\tchecksums of §8's lowerings.\n   473\t\n   474\t## 8. Derivations from A\n   475\t\n   476\tEverything in this section is **derived, never persisted in A**, and\n   477\tevery derivation is a deterministic function of A under a pinned\n   478\talgorithm, so that a receiver can recompute and verify it.\n   479\t\n   480\t### 8.1 Static facts per pc: def, use, live\n   481\t\n   482\tFor each body, from the vector alone:\n   483\t\n   484\t- `def(pc)` = `{rd}` for a value-producing instruction, `{}` otherwise;\n   485\t  `use(pc)` = its register operands.\n   486\t- `L(pc)`, the **live-in set** at `pc`: registers read at or after `pc`\n   487\t  on some path without an intervening write, computed by standard\n   488\t  backward liveness over the body's structured control flow (§3.1 gives\n   489\t  the only branching form, so the fixed point is reached in one pass\n   490\t  per nesting level). At a `:return`/`:halt`, `L` is `{value-reg}`; at\n   491\t  a tail `:call`, `L` after it is `{}`.\n   492\t- The **window rule**: a frame or capture at resume pc `p` carries\n   493\t  exactly `W↾L(p)`, and `rd ∉ L(p)` always holds for the instruction at\n   494\t  `p−1` that delivers to `rd`, because `rd` is defined, not used, by the\n   495\t  delivery.\n   496\t\n   497\tThese replace UCF §7.4.2's `stack-effect` and `lexically-required`\n   498\tstatic facts; `lexically-required` (names a pc can read from `E`) is\n   499\tunchanged.\n   500\t\n   501\t### 8.2 A → R: the de Bruijn register image\n   502\t\n   503\t1. **Resolve on the vector.** Build the closure-owner tree: each\n   504\t   `:closure rd params body-pc` is the unique site owning body `body-pc`\n   505\t   (§3.4 rule 6), so every body has one enclosing chain of parameter\n   506\t   vectors. Rewrite `[:var rd name]` to `[:load-bound rd depth position]`\n   507\t   when `name` is bound in the chain (innermost first), else\n   508\t   `[:load-free rd name]`; rewrite `[:closure rd params body-pc]` to\n   509\t   `[:closure rd arity body-pc]`; names go to the diagnostic side table.\n   510\t   This is `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` applied per `:var`, and the\n   511\t   output is a stage value with no identity.\n   512\t2. **Allocate per body.** Virtual registers are definition-ordered by\n   513\t   construction (pre-order minting). Linear scan: process pcs in order;\n   514\t   at a definition assign the lowest physical temporary `Tᵢ` not live;\n   515\t   a register's lifetime ends at its last use (from §8.1); the two\n   516\t   exclusive arm definitions of one virtual register receive the same\n   517\t   physical register (they are one lifetime); parameters are `L0..Lₙ₋₁`\n   518\t   and are never coalesced with temporaries (register design §4.2).\n   519\t   Tie-breaks: lowest virtual id. Physical count = `n + max live\n   520\t   temporaries`, recorded in the body descriptor.\n   521\t3. **Fill `live`.** For every boundary instruction (`:call`, stream\n   522\t   ops, `:ffi-call`, `:current-continuation`, `:park`), the `live`\n   523\t   operand is `L(pc+1)` mapped to physical registers, which is what the\n   524\t   register design's `body-liveness` computes today.\n   525\t4. **Layout.** Bodies and pcs keep A's layout; the register image's\n   526\t   `{:bodies … :instructions …}` shape is filled from the body ranges.\n   527\t   R = `register-hash` of the result, as a checksum.\n   528\t\n   529\tWhat this replaces in R1 (register design §4.1-4.6): the input\n   530\tvalidation (now §3.4), the expression-tree walk and its\n   531\ttarget-register-passing recursion (now steps 1-2 over a vector), and\n   532\tthe consumption-event allocator (now explicit last-use linear scan).\n   533\t`body-liveness` and the R2 descriptor are reused. The register image's\n   534\t`lift` changes its output contract to the §2.3 table.\n   535\t\n   536\t### 8.3 A → H: the de Bruijn stack image\n   537\t\n   538\t1. Resolve as in §8.2 step 1.\n   539\t2. **Parse** each body under §3.1 into its expression tree; the\n   540\t   validator has already established that it parses.\n   541\t3. **Emit** the tree with the existing `lower-stack` walk\n   542\t   (`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`), which is the named linearizer's\n   543\t   flattening: operator, `:push`, operands with `:push` each, `:call\n   544\t   argc tail?`, labels for conditionals, bodies out of line in discovery\n   545\t   order. H = `image-hash` of the result, as a checksum.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 18:27:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 01:27:24 Asia/Bangkok\n\nThe architecture is sound in direction, but this draft is not ready to freeze. The main blockers are contradictory window rules, an incomplete grammar, and an incomplete transport shape for tail effects. Read-only review; no files edited or tests run.\n\nReferences below are to `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` unless otherwise stated.\n\n| Severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | Recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High — architectural defect | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:492` | Standard successor live-in includes the result destination when subsequent instructions read it. For `[:park 0] [:halt 0]`, `L(1) = {0}`, contradicting `rd ∉ L(p)` and the window refusals. | Keep standard liveness. Define the saved window as `W↾(L(p) − {rd})` for write-result delivery; use that rule consistently for calls, captures, waits, reconstruction, and R live operands. |\n| High — architectural defect | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:262` | The grammar has no production for `gensym`, `store-get`, or scalar `store-put`; stream-make’s zero-expression production is unspecified. | Publish an exhaustive production for every opcode, with exact child count/order and literal operands. |\n| High — architectural defect | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:265` | An `if` expression does not end with a value-writing instruction. Nested conditionals can give one destination three or more static definitions, contradicting §3.2’s pairwise rule. | Define `expr-into(rd)` recursively as an expression with result destination `rd`. Validate exclusive definitions by structured paths, allowing recursively nested alternatives. |\n| High — architectural defect | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:311` | Tail calls inside conditional arms are followed textually by structural jumps/alternate code. Pure primitives explicitly continue at `pc+1`, so their structural return is reachable. | Separate structural layout from runtime successors. Either make every tail call return its result directly, including immediate primitive results, or specify the differing paths honestly. |\n| High — specification gap, before freeze | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:374` | Tail-effect waits have no current activation or resume PC, but §4.2 requires segment, resume PC, E and window. The linker row always specifies write-result delivery. | Define a closed through-return wait/capture variant retaining the suspension site, K, pending data and required store context; distinguish it from a resumable activation. Apply it to tail require waits too. |\n| High — specification gap, before freeze | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:150` | The call cases omit invoking a reified continuation. Today’s `semantic/apply-call` has an explicit abortive `:continuation` arm. | Specify continuation invocation through `:call`: validate arguments/ownership, discard the caller activation and K, restore the capture, and deliver once. |\n| High — architectural defect | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:412` | The proposed `:regs` row drops `k-cref`, although the old row and §4.2 both require K. | Retain K in `:regs`; define delivery as a closed typed shape, including the tail variant. |\n| High — specification gap, before freeze | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:504` | §3.4 rule 6 does not actually require a unique closure site per body or an acyclic owner tree. Resolution assumes both. | Validate complete body partitioning, one owner per non-main body, rooted acyclic ownership, and body/branch target boundaries. |\n| High — specification gap, before phase 3 | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:512` | Pre-order IDs are not definition-ordered PCs—the worked example demonstrates that. “Lowest not live” does not specify allocation across alternative definitions or reservation holes. | Pin intervals/reservations, expiration order, source/destination overlap, shared-destination handling and count calculation in a complete algorithm. |\n| Medium — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:521` | R live operands are not on every stream opcode; existing `body-liveness` excludes the destination and treats tail calls as having no successors. | Name exactly the r2 boundary opcodes and compute their operands using the corrected save rule or the existing physical-image function. |\n| Medium — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:450` | Byte equality alone does not establish unchanged descriptors, validation, allocation or execution contracts. | Retain b2/r2 only when both bytes and normative contracts remain unchanged. Version any changed contract even if corpus goldens happen to match. |\n| Medium — specification gap | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:37` | Equal observable behavior is not a literal state-space isomorphism. Walker expression frames become PCs and windows; administrative steps differ. | Define a correspondence allowing administrative steps, with explicit mappings for each supported walker frame and effect phase. |\n| Low — editorial | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:603` | Phase 1 is described as in flight, although the identity amendments have merged. | Mark phase 1 complete. |\n\n1. **The six required changes**\n\n   - **A-only identity:** addressed by the merged amendments and §§1, 7.\n   - **Structured grammar and minting:** partially addressed by §3. Pre-order and body-local scope are good choices, but productions, recursive branch definitions, owner validation and terminal paths need correction.\n   - **Windows and delivery:** partially addressed by §§2.4, 4, 8.1. Destination subtraction and the tail-state grammar are blockers.\n   - **Explicit derivations:** partially addressed by §8. Whole-body stack lowering is correctly chosen; allocation remains underspecified.\n   - **Versions and byte evidence:** partially addressed by §§6, 8.4. The decision must include contract changes.\n   - **Stage D/E gates before deletion:** addressed in §10’s intent. Move implementation of the corrected liveness rule earlier and strengthen transport gates as described below.\n\n2. **Isomorphism**\n\n   The intended correspondence works for supported evaluation behavior, but the stated one-register/one-frame correspondence is too literal.\n\n   - `eval-operator` and `eval-operand` become instruction position plus operator/argument registers.\n   - `eval-test` becomes test evaluation followed by `branch-false`.\n   - `eval-stream-put-target` becomes the PC before value evaluation; `eval-stream-put-val` corresponds to the retained target register and computed value.\n   - Stream source/cursor frames become PCs before the relevant instruction.\n   - `eval-define` and `eval-resume-val` become child evaluation followed by their instructions.\n   - FFI `request-sent` is a retained-request phase that transitions to response waiting without delivering the request append result to guest code.\n   - `eval-call` corresponds to correlated response decoding, parked bookkeeping removal, and final result delivery.\n\n   These expression frames are generally represented by **PC and W**, not by a semantic return frame. A return frame represents the suspended caller activation.\n\n   Walker administrative states have no unique one-step register counterpart; register branch/jump/return states likewise need not match a walker step individually. This is acceptable with a correspondence allowing administrative transitions.\n\n   The missing semantic `:call` continuation case is a real gap. Also scope the claim to the shared supported AST vocabulary: the walker contains a `store-update` arm, while current lowering explicitly rejects it.\n\n3. **Grammar and minting**\n\n   The grammar is incomplete and internally contradictory as listed above. Make opcode productions exhaustive and replace “last instruction writes rd” with a recursively defined result relation.\n\n   Pre-order minting is suitable, but specify:\n\n   - Supplying an arm destination does **not** mint another root destination.\n   - A resume expression has no result destination: either mint none or explicitly allow an unused ID. The current “every expression” and “every ID has a definition” rules conflict here.\n   - Zero-operand operations, define’s literal name, defaults and child order.\n   - Queue order for lambdas discovered while emitting queued bodies.\n   - Expansion of shared AST occurrences; no implicit body deduplication.\n\n   Body-local IDs work with absolute body PCs when ownership is validated. Definite assignment is decidable by a structured forward walk: intersect assigned sets from continuing arms, and exclude terminal arms from the join. Structural unreachable instructions require a separate validation policy.\n\n4. **Delivery**\n\n   Direct through-K delivery is viable. The engine seam supplies `restore-fn(base, entry, value)` and treats VM payload as data; it does not require a current activation. A new semantic restore can decode the completion, pop one return frame, write its destination, or halt with the result when K is empty.\n\n   No code trampoline is required. This still needs an explicit restore transition, including immediate effect completion, terminal errors, FFI decoding and cleanup.\n\n   Complete the call cases for closures, ordinary primitive values, effect values, reified continuations and non-callable errors. Make pure and effectful tail completion consistent.\n\n   `request-sent` must advance the FFI protocol without guest result delivery. Link request/response/install phases similarly preserve delivery until final completion. Halt transports a result; explicit park transports a resumable record.\n\n5. **Windows**\n\n   Standard `L(pc)` is well-defined over an explicit CFG, including unreachable structural instructions. The draft incorrectly equates a terminal call’s empty live-out with the live-in set of its textual successor.\n\n   Define:\n\n   `saved(write-result, p, rd) = L(p) − {rd}`\n\n   Through-return has no current activation window; its saved caller windows remain in K.\n\n   This fixes park, reification, non-tail calls, FFI and retained writes. A retained write’s retry value belongs in pending data even when absent from the window.\n\n   The three window refusals are useful membership diagnostics, but insufficient as the full validator. Also check representation, integer IDs, duplicate/order rules for rows, body scope, site/PC pairing, destination equality, delivery mode, return-frame sites, K structure and pending compatibility.\n\n6. **Identity**\n\n   A is deterministic under the intended pinned walk. Remaining inputs to pin are defaults/saturation, occurrence expansion, body queue order, resume minting, and recursive arm destination reuse.\n\n   Require canonical numbering validation—or a project/parse round-trip law—if “canonical vector” means the unique projection, rather than any expression-structured vector with arbitrary contiguous IDs.\n\n   Exact identity is preserved: named binders and free names remain in A; allocation stays outside it. Alpha-renaming may preserve H/R while changing A.\n\n7. **Derivations**\n\n   §8.2 is not sufficiently specified for cross-host implementation. In particular, a shared destination’s allocation must not collide with values computed in its alternate arm. Decide whether to reserve one physical slot across the entire structured interval or use another explicitly pinned method.\n\n   The existing parameter L bank remains reserved; canonical parameters stay in E. Preserve duplicate-parameter resolution, nil-fill and extra-argument behavior.\n\n   §8.3 makes H byte identity plausible. It needs an adapter from recovered expressions to the existing lowerer’s resolved-tuple input, preserving occurrence expansion, defaults, tail flags and body discovery order. Absolute PCs must be recomputed after stack emission.\n\n   §8.4 is honest about likely R changes, but compare actual vectors/encoded bytes as well as checksums, and include normative contract equivalence in the version decision.\n\n8. **Safepoints**\n\n   The operation families are covered. Corrections needed:\n\n   - Split linker phases: request append and successful response processing do not themselves deliver the module symbol; install completion does.\n   - Let every linker phase carry either non-tail write-result or tail through-return delivery.\n   - Explicit park has **no pending wait**; represent it separately from `:wait`.\n   - Reified current-continuation is a captured value, never a task safepoint.\n   - Preserve retained FFI request → response-wait transitions and correlated errors.\n   - Restate whole-task quiescence and existing refusal conditions, including unsupported observation states and pending close.\n\n9. **Blast radius and migration**\n\n   Add explicit coverage for `yin.vm` contract/schema declarations, de Bruijn resolver/lowerer adapters, register-code allocation/validation, pipeline composition, linker derivation verification, dependency census, closure markers and reified continuation encoding.\n\n   “Admission untouched” should mean custody/resource policy; executable admission changes under phase 1. The native de Bruijn code documentation is only a focused edit if its contract survives §8.4.\n\n   Correct the phase dependency: §8.1 liveness is needed by the phase-4 evaluator’s call saves and captures, so implement it with the phase-3 validator/static analysis.\n\n   Phase 6 should gate canonical reconstruction from all intended engines, not only semantic-profile round trips. Phase 7 then reruns Stage D/E custody, crashes and cross-process behavior. Keep deletion gated on all of these.\n\n10. **The four open questions**\n\n   1. **Structural return:** keep it. Define it as layout syntax, not invariably unreachable runtime code, unless all tail primitive completions return directly. Permit structural branch delimiters after terminal arms.\n   2. **Body-local IDs:** keep them. Require validated body ownership and scope.\n   3. **Carried live claim:** recompute. Window keys already provide the membership claim to validate; an additional live vector duplicates it.\n   4. **Tail effects:** route directly through K. The engine restore interface supports this; specify the closed tail wait shape and semantic restore helper. No instruction trampoline is needed.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED — 1) correct liveness and saved windows; 2) complete recursive grammar, terminal paths and owner validation; 3) complete call/delivery and tail transport shapes; 4) restore K in row registers; 5) pin allocation and version decisions; 6) tighten correspondence and migration gates."}}
